  <bioghist id="180309a1d3e1bd39fce3f7601aba39f6">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Joseph Schull started at the Ford Foundation in 1991 as an assistant program officer in the International Affairs program. By 1993, he was promoted to deputy director of the International Affairs program. In 1996, Ford Foundation program areas were restructured, and he became deputy director of the Governance and Civil Society program within the Peace and Social Justice division. The Governance and Civil Society program's overarching goals were to improve governmental performance and accountability, find new approaches to implement public policies, and enhance the role of civic organizations in promoting democratic values. Schull worked to fund new initiatives in Russia and Eastern Europe. Schull left the Ford Foundation for Warburg Pincus in 1998.</p>  </bioghist>
  <scopecontent id="e0332695447001bfb9e0382ed98d0683">
    <head>Scope and Contents</head>
<p>Joseph Schull served as a program officer and deputy director of the International Affairs and the Governance and Civil Society programs at the Ford Foundation between 1991 and 1998. His work focused on creating new foundation initiatives in Russia and Eastern Europe. The collection is comprised of trip reports, articles on civil society issues, Ford program review materials, background papers, policy documents, and conference materials.</p>  </scopecontent>
  <acqinfo id="e9834270409393d11072365e1271c49d">
    <head>Immediate Source of Acquisition</head>
<p>This material was transferred to RAC in 2019 by the Ford Foundation and ingested by RAC as Accession 2019:115.</p>  </acqinfo>
